  "title": "A Fano atom grown at a knot sphere's dissonance",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Splicing a Fano atom (the Fano plane less a point, uniform signs) at a line of a knot sphere along one of the three bijections that keep both rotations gives a knot space on a torus, and the old Kasteleyn signs extend to the new sides with no even face uniquely up to gauge (proved). On every grown torus tested (288 from K_2-K_4, 252 from K_5) the atom acts in the grown class as the line it replaced and is transparent in the other three (computed), so the grown law is the only unsatisfiable class: growth turns a count-forced dissonance into a class-forced one (proved given that). Learning at the dissonance then resolves it, and every resolution changes a sign in the atom or the new sides (proved). Computed: grown at the dissonance, the violated line moves into the atom and the cheapest resolving walk costs 3 on K_2-K_5; grown farther away it costs more; random moves, sight of radius 3 and the outside distance all resolve every start.",
